{
  "text": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its induced norm and Borel σ-algebra, and let λ be its canonical Lebesgue measure normalized by orthonormal coordinates. Put d = finrank_ℝ(E). For every V : E → ℝ and every α,η ∈ ℝ, assume α > 0, η > 0, V is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable everywhere, and\n\nα ‖v‖² ≤ D²V(x)[v,v] for every x,v ∈ E.\n\nDefine the following quantities, which are not additional assumptions:\nZ_V = ∫_E exp(−V(x)) dλ(x);\nμ = Tilt(λ,−V) = λ.withDensity(x ↦ ENNReal.ofReal(exp(−V(x))/Z_V));\nJ = (Φ_η)_*(μ ⊗ γ_E), where Φ_η(x,z) = (x, x + √η · z) and γ_E is the centered standard Gaussian probability measure with identity covariance.\n\nThen all three conclusions hold: (1) Z_V > 0; (2) J has total mass one; and (3), as measures on E × E,\n\nJ = (λ ⊗ λ).withDensity((x,y) ↦ ENNReal.ofReal(((((√(2πη))⁻¹)^d)/Z_V) · exp(−V(x) − ‖y−x‖²/(2η)))).\n\nThe reference measure in this final density is canonical Lebesgue measure in both coordinates. Pushforward assigns to a measurable set its preimage measure, and withDensity assigns the nonnegative integral of the displayed density over that set. The integral defining Z_V is a real totalized Bochner integral, equal to zero if the integrand is not integrable. Tilt uses exactly the displayed normalization and is zero if its exponential weight is not integrable. ENNReal.ofReal takes the nonnegative part of a real number and views it as an extended nonnegative real. Real inverse and division are totalized at zero; η > 0 and the concluded Z_V > 0 make the displayed denominators nonzero. The square root is the nonnegative real square root, π is the circle constant, and exponent d is natural-number power. D²V(x)[v,v] means differentiating the continuous-linear-map-valued first derivative, applying to v, then applying the resulting linear functional to v. Although the derivative API is totalized to zero where differentiability fails, C² regularity supplies actual first and second derivatives here. All derivative hypotheses are global and pointwise. Dimension zero is allowed, with canonical volume and standard Gaussian both unit mass on the one-point space.",
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "Let E be any finite-dimensional real inner-product space with its induced norm and Borel σ-algebra, and let λ be its canonical Lebesgue measure normalized by orthonormal coordinates. Put d = finrank_ℝ(E). For every V : E → ℝ and every α,η ∈ ℝ, assume α > 0, η > 0, V is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable everywhere, and\n\nα ‖v‖² ≤ D²V(x)[v,v] for every x,v ∈ E.\n\nDefine the following quantities, which are not additional assumptions:\nZ_V = ∫_E exp(−V(x)) dλ(x);\nμ = Tilt(λ,−V) = λ.withDensity(x ↦ ENNReal.ofReal(exp(−V(x))/Z_V));\nJ = (Φ_η)_*(μ ⊗ γ_E), where Φ_η(x,z) = (x, x + √η · z) and γ_E is the centered standard Gaussian probability measure with identity covariance.\n\nThen all three conclusions hold: (1) Z_V > 0; (2) J has total mass one; and (3), as measures on E × E,\n\nJ = (λ ⊗ λ).withDensity((x,y) ↦ ENNReal.ofReal(((((√(2πη))⁻¹)^d)/Z_V) · exp(−V(x) − ‖y−x‖²/(2η)))).\n\nThe reference measure in this final density is canonical Lebesgue measure in both coordinates. Pushforward assigns to a measurable set its preimage measure, and withDensity assigns the nonnegative integral of the displayed density over that set. The integral defining Z_V is a real totalized Bochner integral, equal to zero if the integrand is not integrable. Tilt uses exactly the displayed normalization and is zero if its exponential weight is not integrable. ENNReal.ofReal takes the nonnegative part of a real number and views it as an extended nonnegative real. Real inverse and division are totalized at zero; η > 0 and the concluded Z_V > 0 make the displayed denominators nonzero. The square root is the nonnegative real square root, π is the circle constant, and exponent d is natural-number power. D²V(x)[v,v] means differentiating the continuous-linear-map-valued first derivative, applying to v, then applying the resulting linear functional to v. Although the derivative API is totalized to zero where differentiability fails, C² regularity supplies actual first and second derivatives here. All derivative hypotheses are global and pointwise. Dimension zero is allowed, with canonical volume and standard Gaussian both unit mass on the one-point space.",
  "reconstructed_text_sha256": "6E57E0C8D24B0CADD986DDA63A058241D957DB5FF7F156C48178B96B3FD8A1DF",
  "objects": "E, a finite-dimensional real inner-product space; V : E → ℝ; positive real α and η; canonical volume λ; dimension d; standard Gaussian γ_E; the specified let-bound real integral Z_V, tilted measure μ on E, and pushforward measure J on E × E.",
  "domains": "V and all derivatives are defined on E with its induced norm and Borel structure. Z_V is real-valued. μ and γ_E are measures on E, and J is a measure on the product Borel space E × E. The final density is relative to λ ⊗ λ, not μ ⊗ λ or an arbitrary volume.",
  "quantifiers": "For every such E, every V : E → ℝ, and all real α,η satisfying the stated hypotheses. The curvature inequality is required for every point x ∈ E and every direction v ∈ E. The density equality is equality of measures on all measurable sets of E × E.",
  "assumptions": "α > 0; η > 0; V is globally C² over ℝ; and α ‖v‖² ≤ D²V(x)[v,v] for every x,v ∈ E. Finite dimensionality, real inner-product structure, and the Borel measurable structure are imposed on E. No positive-dimension assumption or independent measure input is imposed.",
  "conclusion": "For the specified let-bindings, the conjunction is: Z_V > 0; J is a probability measure; and J equals canonical product volume weighted by ENNReal.ofReal(((((√(2πη))⁻¹)^d)/Z_V) · exp(−V(x)−‖y−x‖²/(2η))).",
  "scopes": "All regularity and curvature hypotheses hold globally and pointwise, not almost everywhere. Z_V, μ, and J are fixed by local definitions, not quantified witnesses or extra premises. The reconstruction asserts only the three displayed conclusions; it does not add a separate conclusion that μ is a probability measure, a conditional-law identity, or an invariant-law claim.",
  "constant_dependencies": "The curvature lower-bound constant is the supplied α > 0. The supplied η > 0 determines the scaling √η, the denominator 2η, and the Gaussian prefactor ((√(2πη))⁻¹)^d. The dimension is d = finrank_ℝ(E), and the normalization divisor is exactly Z_V = ∫ exp(−V) dλ. No additional constants are introduced.",
